  "name": "HERPES 1 & 2 (HERPES SIMPLEX VIRUS - IgM)",
  "code": "2015",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p><strong><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">NOTE:</span></strong><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Kindly correlate with clinical condition. Positive results to be confirmed by PCR.</span></p>\n<p><br><strong><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">TEST INFORMATION:</span></strong><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Herpes Simplex Virus type 1 &amp; 2 IgG/IgM: Herpes simplex virus (HSV) type 1 is the cause of most orofacial herpes and HSV encephalitis. </span><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Type 2 is primary cause of initial and recurrent genital herpes and neonatal HSV infections. Most neonatal HSV infection are acquired at </span><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">delivery resulting in a disseminated infection or encephalitis. The rate of perinatal infection among infants exposed to the virus is nearly </span><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">50% when mothers have primary HSV disease, but &lt; 5% when mothers have reactivated HSV 2 infection. A negative result however does </span><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">not rule out the infection.</span></p>\n<p><strong><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">LIMITATION OF THE TEST:</span></strong><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">False positive results can occur in Cross reactivity, Presence of rheumatoid factor, Persistence of IgM antibodies several months after </span><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">acute infection., Reactivation of virus. False negative results can occur Immunologically immature host.</span></p>",
